dr. robbert gladder joins us now he's an  emergency room doctor at Lenox Hill  Hospital right here in New York City CBS  News senior national security analyst  Fran Townsend and CBS News law  enforcement analyst Paul violas are also  here good morning doctor glad thank you  so Travis will you talk to dr. David  agus later earlier this one he was  talking about the life and death  decisions that have to be made when so  many people are brought in with this  kind of injury right it's an ethical and  moral dilemma that all physicians all  healthcare providers go through because  we try to save everyone unfortunately  that's not the case the way this process  goes is that the people who need  immediate treatment who have for example  a collapsed lung who can't breathe  they're treated first and they're tagged  red others who are a little bit less  severe that might be bleeding they're a  tear lower and we call those yellow  patients at the scene we have an  elaborate system where it's basically  mass casualty where we tag these  patients we prioritize them and we see  who could be treated first people say a  person would say a head injury who's  barely breathing we try to open their  airway but if there's not much chance of  saving them we have to move on and this  is what we all go through and trying to  understand and really deal with this  because you have seconds doctor that's  correct decision you have a hundred  people on a scene you've got to be quick  you've got to be rapid you look at their  pulse their blood pressure are they  breathing are they awake you look at all  these factors and another hurdle for  emergency room officials we've heard is  it good Samaritans did the right thing  and by putting a lot of these patients  and victims in their own cars and in  taxis and driving into the hospital but  given that you don't necessarily have  the statistics and the stats on on what  these patients are going through and  whether or not they can be saved that's  right but we always appreciate patients  that are brought in by you know by  private people in cars because you know  these are patients that get there  quickly sometimes faster than EMS and  these are situations where we can  quickly intervene one of the issues is  that the security I mean who is this  person bringing you know this individual  in we have to identify them so at the  gate at the triage they have to identify  themselves so we know exactly you know  where they're coming from and what the  situation is at least so with first  responders they can give you some little  nugget about what the condition is  someone who's just bringing somebody in  the car that just says help really  doesn't know exactly what they're  dealing with in most cases exactly and  one thing I do want to mention is that  we learned from Boston while this was a  bombing is that  there are certain maneuvers that help  and one is that using certain  tourniquets or properly a probably turn  tourniquets can be life-saving now  certainly individuals don't carry these  they may use belts they may use you know  other improvised devices but tourniquets  were something that could stem blood  loss because blood loss is one of the  key things and ultimately that is the  issue that kills me and you put pressure  on the wound right away the racks  pressure is always useful but a  tourniquet is much more much more  effective and certainly you know  precautions you know so in terms of  blood being at the scene is a concern  for any private individual and has to be  and that tourniquet can stay on for a  lot longer and you don't have to be  holding it the entire time that's right  a properly applied tourniquet a wide  tourniquet a military tourniquet is the  most effective and that's what EMS  carries and these have been really  life-saving and especially from what we  learn in Boston Carter mentioned this as  well but worth mentioning again the the  request for blood donations in Las Vegas  this morning it's urgent and I would you  know everyone to try to get there donate  its blood is that is the sustenance of  life and we need it and it's really  life-saving and what we've learned from  representative Scalise in his interview  with 60 minutes and CBS was just the  number of surgeries that these victims  are going to possibly have to endure  over the next weeks and even months  because of the type of weapon use  correct right these automatic weapons  cause devastating internal injuries we  may see an entry wound and an exit wound  I may not even see the exit but it's  internally that they wreak havoc they  destroyed the intestines that can  puncture the lungs cause fractures  bleeding in the pelvis  and this is the unknown area where we  will have to do use imaging tests and  try to figure out exactly what's causing  the bleeding many of these patients stay  from you know months in the hospital  infections an issue and called  hypothermia these are all delayed  consequences of such systemic trauma  that's what I learned one of the things  I learned on Nora's piece last night and  60 minutes the amount of blood loss that  there wasn't an exit wound so that they  knew immediately on the scene that this  could be major trouble because of how  the bullet moves inside the body and the  importance of applying pressure  immediately exactly that you mentioned  exactly most of us don't know even  what to do or how to do that well when  you do what you can certainly belts are  helpful you know you try to constrict  the air you put direct pressure without  putting yourself in any risk but  certainly you know when EMS arrives the  tourniquet could be life-saving  how equipped is a hospital to deal with  that many patients coming in at the same  time with a vast array of injuries I  think it's an important part you bring  up they are we drill for this we're  ready for this but and when it happens  you nobody for this well we can be you  know there's a coordinated effort  between the emergency department  leadership surgical leadership a  hospital in terms of their leadership  you know their organizational structure  because we have to clear the ER in other  words we have to make sure that any  patients that are being worked up or  moved to the floors or other areas to  make room for the incoming patients and  how do you coordinate with other  hospitals well there's different  networks we all talk to each other  there's called VHF or very high  frequency networks that you can actually  internally message text or email within  that network and we all communicate and  that goes to police fire and EMS as well  so there you know there are multiple  layers and categories of types of  communication that are really imperative  to this effort and time is of the  essence and you have doctors on call but  when you have something like this take  place in the middle of the night is it  that more pressing and difficult to get  doctors in and wake them up it is it can  be an issue but they're always trauma  surgeons in house always ready to react  always to have an elaborate Network go  out of communication to all you know  communicate and say that we're here  bring us all together nurses doctors you  know respiratory text everyone it's an  entire effort and it's it's a community  as a doctor what did you think when you  first heard this story you know when the  story was first reported it was two  people dead then it jumped up to 20  people and now it's jumped up to 50 and  possibly higher what did you think when  you first heard the details that we've  heard so far I was horrified you know I  always look at the initial reports and  I'm always suspicious because I know it  grows knowing that the amount of  firepower this man had and what it could  do I was very you know concerned that  this could be an incredible incredibly  number more more involved deaths almost  absolutely this was going to be  something that was going to be a massive  issue and dr. Crowder you know we heard  ironically from a hospital official that  they had just been briefed by somebody  from Orlando's hospital that had treated  the wounded from the pulse nightclub  treating you mentioned the Boston  bombing as well  how Corden is it for hospitals around  the country to learn from past  experiences and have the sort of  hands-on expertise from those who  unfortunately had to be there and  oversee them it's incredibly important  and it's what we do we communicate with  each other at conferences you know we  present we demonstrate our methods and  these methods have to be practiced over  and over again you know it's something  we drill on in morning reports we do  emails we communicate we present about  it and this is how hospitals work we  work together and we've learned from  unfortunately these horrible incidences  that we have to you know have these  protocols in place so we can save lives  we're always so thankful for the first  responders very much so and let's talk  about the distance where he was shooting  they said at least 400 yards away  spraying into a large number of people  what does that tell you about the type  of wounds or injuries that the hospitals  are treating well they're gonna see a  lot of wounds caused by shrapnel by  blast effect not necessarily just direct  hit and so the could be burns these  could be secondary effects of the  bullets themselves so you know and there  also could be injuries from people  trying to just you know scatter in the  event head injuries fractures Falls we  don't know the age or the demographics  of the population at the concert and  that's another factor because as you get  older your response from these traumatic  injuries can be you know much much less  and as law enforcement Paul and Fran  when you first heard the story did you  think that it was going to be much  higher than you know when when we were  called in this morning we knew it was  serious we were told that it was two  that were dead and at least you know a  hundred others that were injured but the  more you hear did you all know as law  enforcement people that this was much  worse than it was initially reported  yeah I believe so and I can just say the  reason why that Gail is because when  with as soon as I heard that it was  going into a concert area and the  shooter was shooting at a distance then  you know the worst came to mind at that  point with respect to what the macro  side of his agenda was why did the  distance matter to you Paul well because  it's well planned out it wasn't up close  it's not personal it's it's not like he  had something to do with one particular  person he was going after an entire  group he was looking for a macro message  and that type of thing always speaks to  mass casualty and when we understood it  was an automatic weapon you realize he's  spraying a large crowd right so that  he's got lots of potential targets  and he's just wildly spraying them you  expected the numbers were gonna go up at  one point is that the safety of the  scene has to be secured so when others  want to save other people they have to  you know their life is they're larger at  stake as well so knowing that they can  do that without themselves being at risk  is often a concern so I think that's  something nice to me can we expect the  president to visit the hospital when he  comes Wednesday I don't know that he'll  visit remember it's more than one  hospital and I think it's more likely he  will meet with people outside of the  immediate area of the attack all right  Frank Paul we'll hear more from you soon  dr. Robert gladder from Lenox thanks  very much for your talk later
 

